{"name":"Wyvern Green","hex":"#86a96f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wyvern","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A draconian creature possessing wings, only two legs and usually a barbed tail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wyvern"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wyvern","blocks":[{"text":"The wyvern, sometimes spelled wivern, is a type of mythical dragon with two legs, two wings, and often a pointed tail."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Y_Draig_Aur_Owain_Glynd%C5%B5r.jpg/330px-Y_Draig_Aur_Owain_Glynd%C5%B5r.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wyvern","wikidata":"Q862809"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/F._21r._Wyvern.jpg/500px-F._21r._Wyvern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/F._21r._Wyvern.jpg/500px-F._21r._Wyvern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A wyvern from a fourteenth-century manuscript in the National Library of Wales, Aberystwyth: this example has a second head at the end of its tail.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AF._21r._Wyvern.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Chester_Cathedral_-_Chorgest%C3%BChl_7_Wyvern.jpg/500px-Chester_Cathedral_-_Chorgest%C3%BChl_7_Wyvern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Chester_Cathedral_-_Chorgest%C3%BChl_7_Wyvern.jpg/1280px-Chester_Cathedral_-_Chorgest%C3%BChl_7_Wyvern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Chester_Cathedral_-_Chorgest%C3%BChl_7_Wyvern.jpg/500px-Chester_Cathedral_-_Chorgest%C3%BChl_7_Wyvern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A carved wyvern on the choir stalls of Chester Cathedral in Cheshire, England, c. 1380","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChester_Cathedral_-_Chorgest%C3%BChl_7_Wyvern.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/1st_Duke_of_Marlborough_arms.png/500px-1st_Duke_of_Marlboroug